'''2.''' Suited to its purpose; apposite; pertinent; appropriate; becoming: as, an ''apt'' metaphor.
{{smaller block|<poem>Such ''apt'' and gracious words,
That aged ears play truant at his tales,
And younger hearings are quite ravished.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., ii. 1.}}
<poem>Expert
In fitting ''aptest'' words to things.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, lxxv.}}
<poem>Ludicrous yet ''apt'' citations
Of barbarous law Latin.</poem>
{{right|''Whittier'', Bridal of Pennacook.}}}}
'''3.''' Having a tendency; naturally susceptible; liable; likely: as, wheat on moist land is ''apt'' to blast or be winter-killed.
{{smaller block|It [the harbor] is gay with hundreds of small boats, . . . ''apt'' to be painted green and adorned with pictures.
{{right|''C. D. Warner'', Roundabout Journey, p. 135.}}}}
'''4.''' Inclined; predisposed; disposed customarily; prone; ready: as, one who is too ''apt'' to slander others.
{{smaller block|<poem>’Tis time my hard-mouth’d coursers to control,
''Apt'' to run riot, and transgress the goal.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Pythag. Philos., l. 669.}}
What makes you thoughtless in your conduct, and ''apt'' to run into a thousand little imprudences?
{{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, iv. 3.}}}}
'''5.''' Ready; prompt; quick; unusually intelligent; expert; facile: as, a pupil ''apt'' to learn; an ''apt'' wit.
{{smaller block|<poem>Strong, supple, sinew-corded, ''apt'' at arms.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, v.}}
An ''apt'' taster knows which wine has the novel flavor.
{{right|''Stedman'', Poets of America, p. 289.}}}}

